      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, the derivative of the integral with respect to its upper limit is the integrand evaluated at that limit, \\( \\frac{1}{\\left(x^{2} + 1\\right)^{2} + 1} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  [
                     "Add",
                     [
                        "Power",
                        "x",
                        2
                     ],
                     1
                  ],
                  2
               ],
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The upper limit is \\( x^{2} + 1 \\), so the chain rule multiplies by its derivative: \\( g'(x) = \\frac{1}{\\left(x^{2} + 1\\right)^{2} + 1} \\cdot 2 x \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               2,
               "x"
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  [
                     "Add",
                     [
                        "Power",
                        "x",
                        2
                     ],
                     1
                  ],
                  2
               ],
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = 1 \\) the limit is \\( 2 \\) and its derivative is \\( 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( g'\\left(1\\right) = \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{2}{5} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            2,
            5
         ]
      }
